A man who had kept the fire burning in this workshop for more than twenty years began writing a letter in his final week.

The workshop always smelled the same in the early morning: oily ink, aged paper, and the scent of cooled lead. Typesetting boxes were stacked along the walls up to the ceiling, each with a handwritten label. The letters on the labels had yellowed with age. Next month, this building would be emptied. The machines would be taken away by a scrap dealer, and the type would be weighed and sold by the pound.

The veteran typesetter Han Seo-jin stood before his composing stone that morning as well. His hands at seventy-one trembled. But the tremor would cease only when he picked up the type. His fingertips remembered the shape of each character. He took out a worn fountain pen and wrote the first line on a blank sheet of letter paper.

> Dear Ha-eun,

Ha-eun was his granddaughter. She was a designer in her late twenties who created letters on screens. As a child, she would sit on the floor of this workshop, stacking type as she played. From some point on, it became difficult to even see her face on holidays. Seo-jin thought it must have hurt the child to witness the disappearance of her grandfather's work.

## One: The Workshop's Final Days

> Ha-eun, I am writing this letter to tell you how letters are born. I wish for you to know, when you draw a single curve on your screen, where that curve comes from.

Lead type is a small metal rod, each one carved in reverse. A typesetter reads a mirrored world with his hands. Seo-jin recalled that winter when he was nineteen, just beginning to learn this craft. His master spoke little. Instead, he corrected the angle of Seo-jin's hands as he picked up type a hundred times over.

> You do not press letters. You ask the paper to accept them.

His master's words still echoed in his ears. Back then, he did not understand what they meant. He only understood after he turned forty. There is a moment when the amount of ink, the strength of pressure, and the moisture of the paper all align perfectly. At that moment, a letter does not rest on the paper—it seeps into it.

## Two: Memory and Reflection

The busiest time at the workshop came every autumn. Wedding invitations, books, church bulletins from the neighborhood—they all came pouring in. Seo-jin's wife, Young-sook, would bring him lunch and say things like:

> Your hands only love letters. You never even write me a single letter.

He would laugh it off. Only after his wife had gone did he realize: letters left unwritten are the longest debts of all. In his wife's drawer, he found a wedding invitation he had printed long ago. It bore the marks of hands having touched its edges until they had worn away.

So this time, he decided not to delay.

> Ha-eun, if I said I am not sad to see these machines disappear, I would be lying. But the real sadness is something else. The knowledge in these hands will end without being passed on to anyone. The thickness of type, the spacing between lines as if they are breathing, the air to be left between letter and letter. I heard you call it 'kerning.' The name is different, but it is the same thing.

He suddenly stopped writing. Outside the window, a truck was starting its engine. The sound soon faded away. Seo-jin began writing again.

## Three: Hope for the Future

On the final evening, Seo-jin carefully selected and wrapped thirty of the type boxes. The most worn ones, the ones he had treasured most. And he placed the letter on top of them. At the end of the letter, he wrote:

> Do not melt these pieces of type. Keep them. It is fine if you never use them. Just hold them in your hands sometimes, and remember that letters once had weight. Even if the letters you create shine on a screen, if you know that their roots had this kind of weight, then I will believe this workshop has not been in vain.
>
> I love you. Your grandfather.

A few days later, Ha-eun came. When she opened the door, winter sunlight poured long into the empty workshop. Without speaking, she opened a box and picked up a single piece of type. It was cold and heavy in her palm. She studied the reversed letters for a while, then smiled softly.

> Grandfather, I can read this even though it's backwards.

Seo-jin nodded. His eyes were glistening.

> That's right. If you can read it even in a mirror, you can read it anywhere.

Lead disappears. But the love for letters can pass from hand to hand. That evening, Ha-eun arranged pieces of type in a line on her work table and took a photograph. Then she opened her screen and drew the first stroke of a new typeface. At the end of that stroke, a very small, rounded mark remained—like the edge of a piece of lead type.

